L = {}
L["moses-nile"] = """After Joseph died, the family of Israel stayed in Egypt for about four hundred years. They grew from seventy people into a great nation. Then a new king came to power who did not know Joseph. He looked at the Israelites and was afraid. They are too many, he said. If war breaks out they might join our enemies. So he made them slaves. They built his storage cities of Pithom and Rameses with brick and mortar. The Bible says the Egyptians worked them ruthlessly.

But the harder the Egyptians pushed, the more the Israelites grew. So Pharaoh gave an order that was even worse. Every Hebrew baby boy was to be thrown into the Nile. This was a plan to wipe out a whole people.

In the middle of that, a baby was born. His mother was from the tribe of Levi. The Bible says she saw that he was a fine child, and she hid him for three months. We know her name from later in the Bible as Jochebed. His father was Amram, and he had an older sister named Miriam and a brother named Aaron.

When she could no longer hide him, she did something very clever. She got a papyrus basket and coated it with tar and pitch. She put the child in it and set it among the reeds along the bank of the Nile. The Hebrew word for the basket is the same word used for Noah's ark. She had built a tiny ark for her son. She couldn't save him from the water, but she could put him in a boat on it.

Then she left. His sister Miriam stood at a distance to see what would happen to him. Imagine what it took for a mother to walk away. Her faith was not that she knew how it would turn out. It was that she did what she could and left the rest to God. The book of Hebrews says that by faith Moses' parents hid him for three months, and they were not afraid of the king's edict.

Why does this matter? We often think of the Exodus as starting with the plagues. But it began with a mother and a daughter and a basket. God's rescue of a whole nation started with ordinary people refusing to give in to fear. They didn't have the power to stop Pharaoh. They did what was in their hands.

It also matters because of who the baby was. His name was Moses, though that name came a little later. He would become the one who led Israel out of slavery, delivered the law at Sinai, and wrote the first five books of the Bible. Jesus himself is often called a new Moses. And like Moses, Jesus escaped a king who killed baby boys, when Herod did the same thing in Bethlehem.

What happened after? The basket did not go far. As we'll see in the next scene, it floated right to the place where Pharaoh's own daughter came to bathe. The very man who tried to destroy Israel would end up raising its deliverer in his own house.

What does this mean for us today? Maybe you are in a situation that feels out of your control. A child, a family member, or a future you can't protect. Jochebed shows what you can do. You do the next faithful thing, and you put the one you love in God's hands. She didn't have a plan for what happened after the basket. She just trusted.

This story also shows that God works through small acts, and that nothing done in faith is wasted. A basket of reeds, a sister watching, a river. That's all God needed.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that the God who hears the cries of slaves was already at work before anyone saw it. The rescue had started. It was just hidden in the reeds."""

L["moses-found"] = """Moses' mother had put him in a basket in the Nile, and his sister Miriam was watching from a distance. Soon someone came to the river. It was Pharaoh's daughter, with her attendants walking along the bank, and she was coming down to bathe. She saw the basket among the reeds and sent her maid to get it.

When she opened it, she saw a baby crying. The Bible says she felt sorry for him. This is one of the Bible's wonderful twists. The daughter of the man who ordered baby boys to be thrown in the river was moved by the cry of one of them. She said, this is one of the Hebrew babies.

Then Miriam stepped out of the reeds. She asked, shall I go and get one of the Hebrew women to nurse the baby for you? Pharaoh's daughter said, yes, go. So the girl went and got the baby's own mother. Pharaoh's daughter said to her, take this baby and nurse him for me, and I will pay you. So Jochebed took her son home and nursed him, and was paid to do it.

When the child grew older, she took him to Pharaoh's daughter, and he became her son. She named him Moses. The Bible says, she drew him out of the water. That is what the name sounds like in Hebrew. He grew up in the palace. Stephen, in the book of Acts, says he was educated in all the wisdom of the Egyptians and was powerful in speech and action.

Think about what that meant. Moses was raised with a mother who knew who he was, and also with the best education of the greatest empire in the world. He knew he was a Hebrew, and he grew up in Pharaoh's court. God was preparing him for both worlds.

When he was grown, Moses went out to see his own people and saw their hard labor. He saw an Egyptian beating a Hebrew. He looked around, and when he saw no one, he killed the Egyptian and hid his body in the sand. The next day he tried to stop two Hebrews from fighting, and one of them said, who made you ruler and judge over us? Are you thinking of killing me as you killed the Egyptian? Moses was afraid. Then Pharaoh heard about it and tried to kill Moses.

Moses fled to Midian, in the desert far from Egypt. He sat down by a well. There the seven daughters of a priest named Jethro came to water their flock, and some shepherds drove them away. Moses stood up and rescued them and watered their flock. Jethro gave Moses one of his daughters, Zipporah, as a wife. They had a son named Gershom, which sounds like the word for foreigner. I have become a foreigner in a foreign land, he said.

Moses stayed in the desert for forty years, tending sheep. Stephen says he was forty years old when he fled, and the Bible says he was eighty when God spoke to him at the burning bush. So he spent forty years as a prince and forty years as a shepherd before God began to use him.

Why does this matter? It shows that God's timing is slow and careful. Moses had tried to rescue his people in his own strength, and it failed. He needed forty years in the desert to learn that it would be God's strength, not his own. The man who thought he was a somebody in Egypt had to become a nobody before God could use him.

What happened after? One day, Moses saw a bush on fire that did not burn up. God called to him by name. He sent Moses back to the very place he had run from, to free his people. And the man who had been drawn out of the water would lead them through it.

What does this mean for us today? If you feel like you've wasted years, or if you are in a season that looks like a desert, this story says God is not done. Moses' first forty years were spent in a palace, and the next forty tending sheep, and the last forty leading a nation. Not one of those years was wasted.

It also shows that God can work through the very people who oppose him. Pharaoh's own daughter ended up protecting and educating Israel's deliverer.

That is why this story is so important. It tells us that God is already working long before we see it. Rescue was moving toward Israel the whole time. It looked like a basket on a river, and it was God's plan."""

L["let-my-people-go"] = """Moses was eighty years old and herding sheep when God called him from a burning bush. God said he had seen the misery of his people in Egypt, and had heard their cry, and was concerned about their suffering. Then he said, so now, go. I am sending you to Pharaoh to bring my people the Israelites out of Egypt. Moses protested. Who am I? He said he wasn't a good speaker. God told him that his brother Aaron would speak for him, and gave him a name for himself that Moses could take back to Israel. I AM WHO I AM. Tell them, I AM has sent me to you.

Moses and Aaron went to Egypt and gathered the elders of Israel. The people believed them, and when they heard that the Lord was concerned about them and had seen their misery, they bowed down and worshiped. Then Moses and Aaron went to Pharaoh. They said, this is what the Lord, the God of Israel, says. Let my people go, so that they may hold a festival to me in the wilderness.

Pharaoh said, who is the Lord, that I should obey him and let Israel go? I do not know the Lord, and I will not let Israel go. He didn't just say no. He made things worse. That same day he told the slave drivers to stop giving the people straw for making bricks. They would have to gather their own straw, and still make the same number of bricks. The Israelites scattered all over Egypt looking for stubble. They were beaten when they couldn't keep up.

The Israelite foremen came to Pharaoh and complained, and he told them they were lazy. Then they turned on Moses and Aaron. May the Lord look on you and judge you! You have made us a stench to Pharaoh and his officials. Moses was crushed. He went back to the Lord and said, Lord, why have you brought trouble on this people? Is this why you sent me? Ever since I went to Pharaoh to speak in your name, he has brought trouble on this people, and you have not rescued your people at all.

This is the part of the story that is easy to skip. God's rescue began with things getting worse. It's what often happens. The pressure on the people was greater than before, and Moses honestly told God he was disappointed.

God did not scold him. He said, now you will see what I will do to Pharaoh. Because of my mighty hand he will let them go. And then God repeated seven promises. I will bring you out. I will free you. I will redeem you. I will take you as my people. I will be your God. I will bring you to the land. I will give it to you. But when Moses told this to the Israelites, they would not listen to him because of their discouragement and harsh labor.

Why does this matter? It shows that the road to freedom started with a hard no. Pharaoh was not just a stubborn man. In the world of the Bible, he was seen as a god, so this was a contest between the God of Israel and the gods of Egypt. Every plague that follows is aimed at one of them. The Nile, the sun, the cattle, all were things Egypt worshiped.

What happened after? Ten plagues came, one after another. The Nile turned to blood. Frogs, gnats, flies, dead livestock, boils, hail, locusts, darkness, and the death of the firstborn. Each time, Pharaoh would give in a little and then harden his heart. Finally, after the last plague, he let them go.

What does this mean for us today? If you have ever done what God asked and things got worse, you are in good company. Moses did exactly what God said and the result was more pain. Obedience does not always make the road easier, at first.

It also teaches us to be honest with God. Moses told him exactly how he felt, and God didn't reject him. He answered with promises.

That is why this story is so important. It is the beginning of the greatest rescue in the Old Testament. It says that God hears the cries of the oppressed, that no power on earth is too strong for him, and that when things look the worst, he may be just getting started. His words to Pharaoh are still words to every power that holds people down. Let my people go."""

L["darkness"] = """Nine plagues had struck Egypt. The Nile had turned to blood. Frogs, gnats, flies, and a sickness on the livestock had followed, then boils, hail, and locusts. Each time Pharaoh had bargained, promised to let the people go, and then changed his mind. The Bible says that Pharaoh hardened his heart, and at other times it says that God hardened it. The story says both. Pharaoh made his own choice again and again, and in the end God let him have what he wanted.

Now came the ninth plague, the darkness. God told Moses, stretch out your hand toward the sky so that darkness will spread over Egypt, darkness that can be felt. Moses stretched out his hand, and total darkness covered all Egypt for three days. No one could see anyone else or leave their place for three days. Yet all the Israelites had light in the places where they lived.

Think about what this meant. The sun was one of the chief gods of Egypt. Their god Ra was believed to be the one who carried the sun across the sky every day. And now the Lord of Israel had made the sun disappear over the whole country. Their greatest god was powerless against him. The darkness was an attack on everything Egypt trusted, not just an inconvenience.

It was also a picture of what Pharaoh was like on the inside. He wouldn't see the truth. He was in the dark even when the light was shining. The Bible later uses this kind of darkness as a picture of life without God, and of judgment.

Pharaoh summoned Moses. Go, worship the Lord, he said. Even your women and children may go with you. Only leave your flocks and herds behind. Moses said, no. Our livestock too must go with us. Not a hoof is to be left behind. We have to use some of them in worshiping the Lord, and until we get there we will not know what we are to use. Pharaoh was furious. Get out of my sight! Make sure you do not appear before me again. The day you see my face you will die.

Moses answered, just as you say. I will never appear before you again. But first, he said, one more plague was coming. About midnight, every firstborn son in Egypt would die, from the firstborn of Pharaoh to the firstborn of the slave girl. But among the Israelites, not even a dog would bark at anyone. Then you will know that the Lord makes a distinction between Egypt and Israel. And Moses left Pharaoh, burning with anger.

Why does this matter? It shows that nothing is hidden from God, and that he can use even the sun, which seems so solid and sure, as a tool. It also shows how real the choice was. Three days of darkness was a huge warning, and Pharaoh would not listen.

The darkness is also a picture of Good Friday. When Jesus died, darkness covered the whole land from noon until three in the afternoon. The same God who had judged evil in Egypt allowed it to fall on his own Son, so that we would not face it ourselves.

What happened after? The tenth and final plague came. On the night of the Passover, the firstborn of Egypt died, and Pharaoh finally sent Israel away. The people left with silver and gold and clothing that the Egyptians gave them.

What does this mean for us today? In the middle of the darkest night, the people of Israel had light where they lived. God can give light to his people even in the worst place. You may be in a dark time, but you are not alone in it. He knows where you live.

It also warns us that ignoring God doesn't make him go away. The more Pharaoh closed his eyes, the thicker the darkness grew.

That is why this story is so important. It shows that God is over every power we are afraid of, even the sun. And it points ahead to the one who said, I am the light of the world. Whoever follows me will never walk in darkness."""

L["passover"] = """Nine plagues had hit Egypt, and Pharaoh still would not let Israel go. Now God was about to send the last one. Before it came, he gave Moses and Aaron detailed instructions for the people of Israel. This month would be the first month of their year. It was a new beginning.

On the tenth day, each family was to choose a lamb, a year-old male without any defect. They were to keep it until the fourteenth day. For four days it lived in the house with the family. Then at twilight the whole community of Israel was to slaughter their lambs. They were to take some of the blood and put it on the sides and tops of the doorframes of the houses where they ate. That night they were to roast the meat and eat it with bitter herbs and bread made without yeast. They were to eat it in a hurry, with cloaks tucked into their belts, sandals on their feet, and staffs in their hands. It is the Lord's Passover.

God said, on that same night I will pass through Egypt and strike down every firstborn, both people and animals, and I will bring judgment on all the gods of Egypt. I am the Lord. The blood will be a sign for you on the houses where you are, and when I see the blood, I will pass over you. No destructive plague will touch you.

Notice what the blood was for. It wasn't a sign for the people. It was a sign for God. The Israelites weren't spared because they were better than the Egyptians. They were spared because a lamb had died in their place and its blood was on the door.

At midnight, the Lord struck down all the firstborn in Egypt, from the firstborn of Pharaoh, who sat on the throne, to the firstborn of the prisoner in the dungeon, and the firstborn of all the livestock. Pharaoh and all his officials got up during the night, and there was loud wailing in Egypt, for there was not a house without someone dead.

Pharaoh summoned Moses and Aaron during the night. Up! Leave my people, you and the Israelites! Go, worship the Lord as you have requested. Take your flocks and herds, and go. And also bless me. The Egyptians urged the people to hurry and leave. The Israelites asked them for silver, gold, and clothing, and the Egyptians gave them what they asked for.

About 600,000 men on foot, besides women and children, left Egypt that day. A mixed crowd went up with them too, along with flocks and herds. They had been in Egypt 430 years to the day. God told them to remember this day forever, and to celebrate it every year as a festival to the Lord.

Why does this matter? It is the center of the Old Testament. Every year after, Israel kept the Passover to remember how God saved them. And when Jesus came, he was called the Lamb of God. John the Baptist said it when he saw him, look, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world. Jesus was crucified at the time of the Passover. He shared a Passover meal with his disciples on the night before he died, and he said the cup was his blood.

Paul wrote it plainly. Christ, our Passover lamb, has been sacrificed. The lamb without defect, the blood on the door, the people saved by a death in their place. It was all pointing forward to Jesus.

What happened after? The people walked out of Egypt, and at the Red Sea, God brought them through. They became a free nation. But they were not yet home. A long journey in the desert lay ahead.

What does this mean for us today? The Passover tells us that God saves by a substitute. Nobody in Israel earned their way out. They listened, trusted, and put the blood on the door. The only thing they did was believe what God said and act on it.

It also means that when you feel judged or afraid, there is a way of safety. The people inside the houses were not hiding in a perfect house. They were safe because of the blood.

That is why this story is so important. It shows what God does with people who cannot save themselves. He sends a lamb. And for every one of us, he did."""
